vmvga: fill shadow clears at memory speed

An accelerated D3D9 clear also clears the CPU shadow of every target
(the shadow must stay authoritative because eviction discards the host
copy without a readback). That shadow clear stored every pixel through
a byte-by-byte loop: in a Halo Online match, with about nine full-screen
clears per frame on 4- and 8-byte-per-pixel targets, it was 52% of
QEMU's main-thread time (plus most of the 27% in libc).

Full-value fills now build one row by doubling memcpy()s from a single
packed pixel and copy it to the other rows; depth/stencil clears that
replace every bit of the pixel take the same path, and partial
depth/stencil clears keep their read-modify-write with a 4-byte fast
path. Bytes written are identical (randomized comparison against the
old loops, 300,000 cases). Halo Online on Guardian: 31-32 -> 45-52
presents per second.

diff --git a/hw/display/vmware_vga_3d.c b/hw/display/vmware_vga_3d.c
index 99e778c..a15f33f 100644
--- a/hw/display/vmware_vga_3d.c
+++ b/hw/display/vmware_vga_3d.c
@@ -6364,6 +6364,37 @@ static void vmsvga3d_store_le(uint8_t *data, uint32_t bytes,
     }
 }
 
+/*
+ * Fill a clipped rectangle of a shadow image with one packed pixel value.
+ * The first row is built by doubling copies of the first pixel and then
+ * copied to the other rows: the same bytes as a per-pixel store loop,
+ * at memory speed.
+ */
+static void vmsvga3d_fill_rect(VMSVGA3DSurfaceImage *image,
+                               const SVGA3dRect *rect, uint32_t bytes,
+                               uint32_t value)
+{
+    uint8_t *first = image->data + (uint64_t)rect->y * image->pitch +
+                     (uint64_t)rect->x * bytes;
+    size_t row_bytes = (size_t)rect->w * bytes;
+    size_t filled = bytes;
+    uint32_t y;
+
+    if (rect->w == 0 || rect->h == 0) {
+        return;
+    }
+    vmsvga3d_store_le(first, bytes, value);
+    while (filled < row_bytes) {
+        size_t chunk = MIN(filled, row_bytes - filled);
+
+        memcpy(first + filled, first, chunk);
+        filled += chunk;
+    }
+    for (y = 1; y < rect->h; y++) {
+        memcpy(first + (uint64_t)y * image->pitch, first, row_bytes);
+    }
+}
